{"name":"Stone’s Throw","hex":"#605c58","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"},{"term":"throw","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To hurl; to release (an object) with some force from one’s hands, an apparatus, etc. so that it moves rapidly through the air.","To eject or cause to fall off."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of throwing something.","A move in which one lifts or unbalances one’s opponent and then brings him down to the ground."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/throw"}],"entries":[{"title":"Throwing","blocks":[{"text":"Throwing is a physical action that consists of mechanically accelerating a projectile and then releasing it into a ballistic trajectory, usually with the aim of impacting a distant target. Throwing typically refers to hand-throwing by animals with prehensile forelimbs, in which the projectile is grasped in a hand and the proximal limb segments move through compounded kinematic chains to impart a mechanically advantaged swinging motion. For other animals, the definition of throwing is somewhat unclear, as other actions such as spitting or spraying may or may not be included."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/Throwing_rocks_%283843366278%29.jpg/330px-Throwing_rocks_%283843366278%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Throwing"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Ken_Westerfield_Sidearm_throw.jpg/500px-Ken_Westerfield_Sidearm_throw.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Ken_Westerfield_Sidearm_throw.jpg/500px-Ken_Westerfield_Sidearm_throw.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Ken Westerfield sidearm (forehand) frisbee distance throwing, 1970s.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKen_Westerfield_Sidearm_throw.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/MattKataThrowing.jpg/500px-MattKataThrowing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/MattKataThrowing.jpg/1280px-MattKataThrowing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/MattKataThrowing.jpg/500px-MattKataThrowing.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Throwing of a baseball","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMattKataThrowing.jpg"},{"s